{
 "type": "FeatureCollection",
 "metadata": {
  "note": "The Monongahela Railway was a coal road, jointly owned by the Pennsylvania, the P&LE (New York Central) and, from 1927, the B&O - but it did carry passengers, and it gave Morgantown its second station. Its line ran up the WEST bank of the Monongahela, opposite the B&O, from Fairmont through Morgantown and Point Marion to Brownsville, Pennsylvania. Passenger service began in 1903, reached Fairmont by 1915, went over to a gas-electric doodlebug in 1940 and ended on 21 October 1950. RULE 1 NOTE: this layer is drawn only for the passenger years. The Monongahela hauled coal here long after 1950 - it lasted until Conrail bought it in 1993 - and the modern NS Loveridge Secondary is freight only. Do not extend these segments past 1950 on the strength of the railroad still existing.",
  "sources": [
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monongahela_Railway",
   "https://www.american-rails.com/mga.html",
   "https://onview.lib.wvu.edu/catalog/033806"
  ],
  "not_yet_sourced": [
   "train frequencies and schedules in every era - the headways here are estimates",
   "which stretches were open to passengers in 1903 (1903 is the start of Monongahela passenger service generally)",
   "the location and opening year of the Monongahela's Morgantown passenger station (a WVU photograph records it, but no date was checked)"
  ]
 },
