    "avg_speed_kmh": 35,
    "speed_source": "estimated: a coal road's local passenger train, latterly a gas-electric doodlebug; unsourced",
    "geometry_quality": "real-traced from OpenStreetMap ways tagged old_railway_operator=Monongahela - the still-active NS Loveridge Secondary north from Fairmont and the abandoned grade on the west bank opposite Morgantown. 39.6 mi traced Fairmont - Point Marion; straight-line bridging on the whole Fairmont - Brownsville trace totals 0.83 km over five connectors, the longest 298 m.",
    "segments": [
     {
      "start": 1915,
      "end": 1950,
      "headway_min": 600,
      "note": "The Monongahela Railway reached Morgantown on the WEST bank of the river - rails laid there in 1911 by the Buckhannon & Northern, which became part of the Monongahela - and its passenger trains were extended south to Fairmont by 1915, giving Morgantown a second passenger station, the Monongahela's own, across the river from the B&O. A gas-electric doodlebug took over in 1940 and lasted only a few years. The Monongahela was granted permission to drop its remaining Fairmont - Brownsville train and ended passenger service on 21 October 1950. It stayed a heavy coal hauler for decades afterwards; only the passenger years are drawn here.",
      "sources": [
       "Segment-level: the 1911 west-bank construction, passenger service reaching Fairmont by 1915, the 1940 doodlebug and the 21 October 1950 end of passenger service are from American-Rails and the Wikipedia article on the Monongahela Railway. The headway is an estimate and is not sourced.",
       "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monongahela_Railway",
       "https://www.american-rails.com/mga.html"
      ]
     }
    ]
   },
